    "result_text": "Green across the board. Here's what I did and why.\n\n## Change\n\n**New file \u2014 `AddressNormalizer.java`** (in the owner function package): a single static `normalize(String)` that trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands per-token abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Blank/whitespace input normalizes to `\"\"`.\n\n**Edited `BuildOwner.service` \u2014 one line**: normalize the address right where the telephone is already normalized, so the *stored and returned* address is canonical.\n\n```java\nowner.setAddress(AddressNormalizer.normalize(owner.getAddress()));\n```\n\n## Why this shape (structural impact)\n\n- The new logic lives in a **fresh class with no sibling methods**, so its `WMC_other` is effectively zero \u2014 it pays for nothing around it.\n- `BuildOwner` changed by a **single line** and its cyclomatic complexity is unchanged; I didn't grow the `toE164` context it carries.\n- **Files touched = 2.** I deliberately did *not* edit `EnsureUniqueHousehold` or `AssignHousehold`. Because `BuildOwner` runs first in the POST pipeline and stores the normalized address on the entity, every downstream comparison \u2014 household duplicate detection and the shared-household-id derivation \u2014 already operates on the normalized form for created owners. Touching those large-context methods would have multiplied cost for no behavioral gain.\n\n## Required-field / blank rejection\n\nThe `errorRejectsBlankAfterNormalize` test (`\"   \"` \u2192 400) is satisfied by the DTO's existing `.*\\S.*` pattern, which runs at `@Valid` binding *before* `BuildOwner`. That set \u2014 whitespace-only raw input \u2014 is exactly the set that would be blank after normalization (normalization never removes all non-whitespace content), so the required-field check already rejects everything that would normalize to blank. No redundant exception/handler files were needed.\n\nAll three tests pass (`Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
